WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.816
Ten years ago, this village in the Ardèche still had a bakery, a bar-tobacconist and a two-class school. Then the bakery closed, because no one took it over, and the school lost a class. As in many rural communes, residents did their shopping at the supermarket twenty kilometres away, and the central square stood empty most of the time.

00:00:21.066 --> 00:00:44.970
It was a young market gardener, recently settled there, who had the idea of reviving a Saturday-morning market. 'At the start, there were four of us producers and about thirty customers,' she says. 'I thought it would last one summer.' Today, some twenty stallholders come every week: cheesemakers, beekeepers, a baker who bakes his bread in a wood-fired oven, and even a bicycle repairer.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:01:05.376
The whole thing runs on an association of volunteers. Every Saturday they put up the gazebos at six in the morning, allocate the pitches and run a refreshment stall whose profits pay for events: a concert in June, a car-boot sale in the autumn. 'Nobody gets paid, but everybody gains something,' the treasurer sums up.

00:01:05.626 --> 00:01:22.659
Success isn't measured only in turnover. The market has become the place where people meet up, where newcomers meet long-standing residents, where children run between the stalls while their parents chat. The bar, which was thinking of closing, now opens on Saturdays from seven o'clock.

00:01:22.909 --> 00:01:44.016
Not everything is solved, however. Prices remain higher than in supermarkets, and some residents on modest incomes still do most of their shopping elsewhere. The town hall is considering a voucher scheme to make the market accessible to everyone. Because a meeting place that leaves out part of the village, the mayor stresses, would be only half a success.
